      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7523713213952094210/",
      "text": "What if the people businesses are reluctant to hire are some of the talent they’re missing out on? There can be a perception that employing someone who has experienced homelessness, or is at risk of it, will require more time, support and investment. , CEO ofand UK Chair of Homewards’ New Employment Opportunities Network, believes employers should look at the other side of that equation. With the right recruitment, onboarding and ongoing support, businesses can open themselves up to a more diverse pool of talent, help people stay and progress in work and strengthen retention. That matters to individual businesses. But it matters to the economy too. With around one in eight young people not in education, employment or training, businesses have an opportunity to be part of the solution by giving people who might otherwise be overlooked a genuine route into sustainable work. NEON exists to help make that easier, connecting employers to local organisations that support both the business and the young person before and after they start work. sat down withto discuss why this isn’t simply about social responsibility. It’s about seeing potential where others see risk. Full episode in the comments 👇 Follow, hosted by, for conversations about the future of work and careers nobody else is having. … more",
