      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7524053395201118208/",
      "text": "When a new responsibility emerges, external recruitment often becomes the first option. But it's worth asking another question: Does someone on the existing team already have the foundation to take it on? An employee may have developed relevant skills through a previous project. Another may be ready for greater responsibility but hasn't had the opportunity to demonstrate it. Internal talent won't fill every vacancy. Some roles require specialist expertise or experience the organization doesn't currently have. Still, overlooking existing capabilities can lead to unnecessary recruitment costs and missed development opportunities. Before hiring externally, review the skills already available. Consider employees' experience, interests, demonstrated performance, and readiness to learn. Then identify where training or support could bridge the gap. A stronger workforce strategy considers both the talent you can attract and the talent you can develop. … more",
