      "id": "LI00616",
      "sourceId": "LI00616",
      "url": null,
      "text": "The tech industry is going through another major wave of layoffs in 2026. And this time, AI is becoming a bigger part of the conversation. Companies that once hired aggressively are now restructuring their teams, reducing costs, and changing how work gets done. Look at some of the major announcements: • Oracle – 21,000 employees • Amazon – 16,000 employees • Dell – 11,000 employees • Meta – 8,000 employees • HP – Around 6,000 employees • Microsoft – 4,800 employees • PayPal – 4,760 employees • Cisco – 4,000 employees • Block – 4,000 employees • Uber – 3,300 employees • Intuit – 3,000 employees • Visa – 2,600 employees 🌍 BONUS: 3 WEBSITES TO FIND REMOTE JOBS Save these resources for your next job search. 1. Remotive – Curated remote tech opportunities. 🔗 [link hidden] 2. Eztrackr – Build your resume and track applications. 🔗 [link hidden] 3. Remote Rocketship – Discover remote jobs and company insights. 🔗 [link hidden] 🎓 BONUS: 5 COURSES TO UPGRADE YOUR SKILLS IN 2026 Google Introduction to Generative AI [link hidden] Foundations of Project Management [link hidden] Google Project Management [link hidden] IBM Python for Data Science, AI & Development [link hidden] Google Digital Marketing & E-commerce [link hidden] There is a bigger shift happening behind these numbers. Companies are investing heavily in AI infrastructure, automation, and AI-powered productivity tools. At the same time, many are looking to operate with smaller, more efficient teams. But one thing is important to understand. AI isn't simply replacing every job. Businesses are also changing the skills, roles, and capabilities they need. For professionals, this is a reminder to keep learning, adapt to new technologies, and build skills that solve real problems. The future of work is changing faster than many people expected. The real question is: Are we preparing ourselves for the jobs that will exist tomorrow? What's your take on this changing tech job market? Image Credit: Respective Owner … more",
