      "id": "LI00869",
      "sourceId": "LI00869",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7522276334467923968/",
      "text": "In 11 months, I negotiated $71K to $153K as a data analyst in tech My secret is shutting your mouth and not saying any numbers. I never would’ve thought I was worth $140K-153K, so I probably wouldn’t have asked for that much… but multiple companies willingly offered it to me! I realized that saying a number first was me doing their job for them. I was basically telling them exactly how little they could pay me without negotiating. And this way, I was definitely going to lowball myself. Because their budget is almost always more than you think it is. So I stopped saying numbers. Every time a recruiter asked \"what are your salary expectations?\" I'd say: \"I'd love to meet with more members of the team and better understand the role so I can assess the market value. But if you have a budget, I wouldn't mind hearing it.” Then SIT IN THE SILENCE. Don’t fill the awkwardness. Just wait for them to talk. Silence is your power, own it. Companies will almost tell you their budget and if they don’t thats a red flag. Once they named their number, I either said yes, I'm aligned with that range or I moved on. Good negotiation skills literally make you money over time not just in your salary but in your %-based bonuses and raises, 401K contributions, and compounding interest 💅🏻 … more",
