      "id": "LI00880",
      "sourceId": "LI00880",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7523468377156325376/",
      "text": "𝐇𝐢𝐫𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐧𝐨𝐯𝐞𝐫 𝐡𝐚𝐯𝐞 𝐬𝐥𝐨𝐰𝐞𝐝, 𝐛𝐮𝐭 𝐩𝐚𝐲 𝐪𝐮𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧𝐬 𝐡𝐚𝐯𝐞𝐧'𝐭. With the cost of living rising, employees may feel their wages aren't keeping up, even when their pay aligns with market rates. Employers are budgeting about 3.5% on average for salary increases. Pay transparency laws now cover more than 35% of the U.S. workforce. That makes it more important for managers to be ready for these conversations. Managers should be prepared for questions like: • 𝘞𝘩𝘺 𝘢𝘮 𝘐 𝘯𝘰𝘵 𝘨𝘦𝘵𝘵𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘢 𝘳𝘢𝘪𝘴𝘦? • 𝘞𝘩𝘺 𝘢𝘮 𝘐 𝘢𝘵 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘭𝘰𝘸 𝘦𝘯𝘥 𝘰𝘧 𝘮𝘺 𝘱𝘢𝘺 𝘳𝘢𝘯𝘨𝘦? • 𝘞𝘩𝘺 𝘪𝘴 𝘮𝘺 𝘳𝘢𝘯𝘨𝘦 𝘭𝘰𝘸𝘦𝘳 𝘵𝘩𝘢𝘯 𝘮𝘺 𝘤𝘰𝘭𝘭𝘦𝘢𝘨𝘶𝘦'𝘴? • 𝘊𝘢𝘯 𝘐 𝘴𝘦𝘦 𝘸𝘩𝘢𝘵 𝘰𝘵𝘩𝘦𝘳𝘴 𝘢𝘳𝘦 𝘮𝘢𝘬𝘪𝘯𝘨? A clear compensation philosophy, a sound pay structure, and benchmarking with verified market data give managers the facts they need. They can then acknowledge concerns, stay focused on the facts, avoid promises about future raises, and close on a constructive note. Our latest Executive Insights article includes a five-step discussion framework to help managers lead these conversations with confidence. Read more: [link hidden] #PayTransparency #Benchmarking #EmployeeEngagement #DifficultConversations #USI … more",
