      "id": "LI00888",
      "sourceId": "LI00888",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7524081877218054144/",
      "text": "💫Pay transparency isn't just about compliance💫 Most employers say they want to pay people fairly. Fewer are fully confident that their own pay arrangements would hold up if someone looked closely.⬇️ Over time, organisations change. Roles grow, businesses merge and salaries get agreed in very different markets. Each decision might have made sense at the time. Taken together, they can be hard to explain. The EU Pay Transparency Directive and the government's recent consultation on equal pay are bringing this into the open. Our autumn Reward Management Survey suggests it's already changing how organisations think about pay, with an increased push for transparency. Why is one role paid more than another? Why are two people doing similar jobs on different salaries? What determines whether someone progresses to the next pay grade? These aren't unreasonable questions. Employees are already asking the questions. Why is my role paid less than that one? Why are two of us doing similar work on different salaries? Publishing pay ranges won't answer those questions by itself. You need a clear, consistent basis for how jobs are valued and that's what job evaluation gives you. It isn't the most exciting topic on the HR agenda but I would argue it's one of the most important right now. You don't have to fix everything at once. A good start is asking: could our managers explain how someone's pay was decided? 📰Read the full blog here: [link hidden] 💭If an employee asked why they're paid what they are, how confident would you be in your answer? #PayTransparency #PayEquity #JobEvaluation #Reward #HRInsights #EqualPay #RewardManagement #PeopleStrategy … more",
