      "id": "LI00899",
      "sourceId": "LI00899",
      "url": null,
      "text": "A candidate can like your company, accept the salary range and still turn down your offer because of the medical benefits. That’s why benchmarking your PEO needs to go deeper than the monthly price. Start with these five things: → Network Are their doctors, specialists and hospitals in it? The same insurance carrier can offer different networks. → Coinsurance After meeting the deductible, how much of the bill does the employee still pay? → Maximum out-of-pocket What could an employee or family have to spend in a bad medical year? What falls outside that limit? → Deductible How much do they pay before the plan starts sharing costs, and which services are covered before that? → Premium What do the company and employee each pay every month? Then check prescription coverage, copays and out-of-network benefits. Those details can change the comparison considerably. Imagine saving an employee $100 a month in premiums while increasing their deductible by $3,000. You’ve lowered their fixed cost. You may also have increased what they’ll spend when they need care. Understand where the savings come from. And remember who has to live with the decision. A candidate whose specialist isn’t in the network or whose child’s medication isn’t covered may choose another employer. Your existing employees are making those comparisons too. At Dinsmore Steele®, we compare the medical offerings alongside the costs so you can see what you’re gaining, what you’re giving up and whether the trade makes sense. A cheaper PEO isn’t a better deal if the savings make your job offers harder to accept. Barbara Pailley Matt Peterson Barbara Pailley Patrick Villalobos Jackie Machado John Cardielle Axalan Victoria Cantu Flor Fustinoni … more",
