      "id": "LI00940",
      "sourceId": "LI00940",
      "url": null,
      "text": "“What’s the compliance rate with Ontario’s pay transparency requirements?” “Are there penalties for not including expected compensation?” These are two of the questions I’ve been asked most often at recent conferences and events. According to the article below, the share of job postings disclosing pay information increased from 49% before January to 71% in June. That’s a 45% increase. A positive sign! But here’s the bigger question: Are we treating pay transparency as a compliance exercise, or as a test of whether our compensation programs are actually defensible? Publishing a salary range doesn’t stop at being transparent externally. It can also expose internal inequities, inconsistent pay decisions, outdated salary structures, and gaps between current employees and new hires. And those issues don’t disappear just because no one raises them openly. What conversations are happening behind closed doors in your organization as pay becomes more transparent? Would love to hear what you’re seeing and hearing. #PayTransparency #TotalRewards #Compensation #PayEquity #HR … more",
