      "id": "LI00950",
      "sourceId": "LI00950",
      "url": null,
      "text": "Your IT salary bands just went public. If you have 50+ staff in B.C., your first pay transparency report was due November 1. Many IT leaders are focused on the report itself. The bigger change is in how you hire. Here’s what I’m seeing: 1. Every public job posting now needs a pay range. Your current team can see what you’d pay a new hire for their job. If those two numbers don’t line up, you’ll hear about it. 2. You can’t ask candidates what they earned before. Your range has to come from current market data, not their last pay cheque. 3. A range that’s too wide looks evasive, and one that’s too low gets no applicants. A posting for a $95K to $160K “Systems Administrator” says you haven’t decided what the role is. Set your range before you post, not after the first round of interviews. IT leaders in Metro Vancouver: has posting pay ranges changed who applies to your roles, or how your team talks about pay? … more",
