      "id": "LI00964",
      "sourceId": "LI00964",
      "url": null,
      "text": "A 9% salary increase budget is approved. One employee receives 3%. Another gets 14%. Someone else gets nothing. All three decisions might be justified. But can the organisation explain why? As we approach 2027, I believe one of the biggest challenges for Total Rewards leaders won't simply be deciding how much to spend on compensation. It will be making pay decisions fair, consistent and explainable. With pay transparency gaining momentum, AI entering compensation decisions and employees asking tougher questions about fairness, traditional salary planning may no longer be enough. In this edition of my Total Rewards Hub newsletter, I explore: ✅ Why a competitive salary budget doesn't automatically create fair pay outcomes. ✅ How pay transparency is changing expectations around accountability. ✅ Why AI-generated compensation recommendations need human judgement. ✅ My proposed Pay Decision Receipt™ framework for making reward decisions more defensible. ✅ What CHROs, CFOs and Reward leaders should reconsider before the 2027 compensation cycle. One question I keep coming back to: If an employee challenges their salary increase six months from now, can we explain the decision with evidence, or would we have to reconstruct the story? That's where I believe the future of Total Rewards is heading. I've shared my perspective in the latest newsletter edition. I'd be interested to hear how other Reward professionals are approaching this. 👇 Read the full article and share your thoughts. 🌐 [link hidden] #TotalRewards #CompensationAndBenefits #PayTransparency #CompensationStrategy #PeopleAnalytics … more",
