      "id": "LI00976",
      "sourceId": "LI00976",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7523787169971421184/",
      "text": "So I made a comment on a post about pay transparency, and boy oh boy did a lot of people have something to say about it. 😂 I'm not sharing the link because I don't need another 50 notifications. But I've been thinking, and I believe I've found an interesting middle ground. Instead of making compensation about what your colleagues earn, what if we made it about what YOU could earn? Here's my proposal: Employees can ask their managers for the FULL salary range of their role. And managers are LEGALLY obligated to provide it, along with an explanation of where the employee sits within that range. ================================ Scenario 1: Top 10% of the salary range Employee: Hey, I'd like to see the pay range for my role, please. Manager: Sure! You're at the 90th percentile. You're approaching the ceiling for this role, so a promotion is probably the best path to a significant increase. Employee: Great! What do I need to do to get promoted? Manager: Let's build a plan with milestones around it. ================================== Scenario 2: Anything below that Employee: Hey, I'd like to see the pay range for my role, please. Manager: Sure! You're currently at the 70th percentile. Employee: Well, I feel I deserve more because of X, Y and Z. Manager: Alright, let's review those points and see whether a compensation adjustment is justified. That's it. No need to know what John from accounting makes. No need to compare yourself with Susan from marketing. Just transparency around your own compensation, where you stand, and what you need to do to earn more. Pay transparency should empower employees to negotiate their worth, not just compare their salaries. What do you think? … more",
