      "id": "LI00983",
      "sourceId": "LI00983",
      "url": null,
      "text": "Transparent salary ranges are becoming law in the EU 💸 Every EU country had to adopt the Pay Transparency Directive by June 7, 2026. Some have already implemented it, others are still drafting the details, but soon it’ll be the new normal across the EU. Here’s what it means: 💶 You’ll know the salary range early enough to negotiate 🙅 Employers aren’t allowed to ask what you earn now or earned before, so your old salary can’t anchor your new offer And here’s the current status of implementation: ✅ Already in force: Italy, Lithuania, Poland, Estonia, Greece, Slovakia, Malta 📅 Planned from January 2027: Netherlands, Denmark, Finland 🛠️ Still in progress: Germany, France, Ireland, Austria, Sweden, Spain, Portugal, Czechia Each country writes its own version, so the details differ. In some countries, like Italy and Poland, the salary has to be in the job opening itself, while in others, like Estonia and Greece, it’s shared before the interview. So before you start interviewing, check how it works in your target country. At Outtalent, fellows also train for offer negotiation, from base salary to bonus and equity. Learn more: [link hidden] Would knowing the pay range upfront change how you apply? How? Share your thoughts in the comments 👇 … more",
