      "id": "LI00989",
      "sourceId": "LI00989",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7523108893460172800/",
      "text": "Pay transparency is putting more pressure on the quality of compensation decisions. Once employees can see the range, they naturally begin asking harder questions about how decisions are made within it. Why am I positioned here? What would change that position? How are exceptions handled? Would another leader make the same call? Those questions are useful because they reveal whether the organization has real clarity behind its compensation practices. A published range can create visibility. It cannot create consistency, explain progression, define manager judgment, or establish the principles behind an individual pay decision. That is why I think the next phase of pay transparency will be less about disclosure and more about decision quality. CompTeam, gradar, and Ellason LLP are conducting the Pay Transparency Practices 2027 Global Research Study to better understand how organizations are handling that shift in practice. If you work in HR, Total Rewards, or Compensation, I would value your perspective. The study takes about 15 minutes, responses are confidential, and participants will receive the full benchmark report to compare their approach with the broader findings. Survey link is in the comments. I am curious: What part of pay transparency creates the most pressure on the organization behind the range: manager judgment, progression, exceptions, or communication? #PayTransparency #CompensationStrategy #TotalRewards #HRLeadership … more",
