      "id": "LI00997",
      "sourceId": "LI00997",
      "url": null,
      "text": "Pay transparency is becoming a bigger part of how employers recruit in Europe, and I wanted to understand what it means for Ireland. The EU Pay Transparency Directive set a deadline of 7 June 2026 for member states to bring it into national law. Ireland did not meet that deadline. Progress is now being made. On 21 October 2026, the Employment Equality and Institutional Burials (Amendment) Bill 2026 passed both Houses of the Oireachtas. It was due to go to the President for consideration, and I couldn't find a commencement date. What I took from it: • Employers with 50 or more employees will report their gender pay gap information through a central Gender Pay Gap Portal. • The public will be able to view and compare employers and sectors. • The Directive also requires employers to share salary ranges with candidates and bans questions about pay history. From what I found, these recruitment rules are not yet law in Ireland. As a first-year HRM student, I'm interested in how this will affect job advertising, pay structures and employer branding. I'd be keen to hear from anyone working in HR or recruitment on how their organisation is preparing. If you work in HR or recruitment, how is your organisation preparing for greater pay transparency? Paula Marie Kilgarriff 基玛琳 #PayTransparency #HumanResources #Recruitment #GenderPayGap #TUDublin Read the article: [link hidden] … more",
