      "sourceId": "LI01004",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7523896919782277120/",
      "text": "Pay transparency laws in 2026 now require you to defend every salary on your books, not just job postings. The shift matters. Job-ad transparency is table stakes. Internal pay structure transparency is different. A regulator or employee can now ask why Person A in the same role makes 18% more than Person B, and \"that's what we hired them at\" doesn't close the gap. The compression risk Neil Vicente flagged in Thomson Reuters' latest Payroll Pulse is real. You don't have to pay everyone the same. You do have to articulate why the differences exist. Experience gap. Promotion timing. Scope creep in the role. Market adjustment when you hired. Performance differential tied to documented outcomes. Those hold up. Gut feel doesn't. For founders and HR-of-one operators running 25 to 500 people, this means an audit before the question arrives. Pull your current roster. Map title to comp. Ask yourself: could I explain this to a lawyer? If the answer is no, you have a comp debt problem that's no longer theoretical. This isn't about compression for its own sake. It's about having a system that survives scrutiny. The companies getting ahead aren't the ones paying everyone equally. They're the ones who can document the logic. What's your current comp structure based on? Title, tenure, market data, performance, or some combination nobody wrote down? #paytransparency #compensation #peopleops #compliance … more",
