      "id": "LI01011",
      "sourceId": "LI01011",
      "url": null,
      "text": "Day 148. Germany's cabinet met on Wednesday. No pay transparency bill, again. Meanwhile Greece's rules started yesterday, and from Thursday employers in Poland carry the burden of proof in unequal pay claims. Here's where all 27 countries stand. 🟢 6 have a law in force 🔵 7 have bills in parliament 🟡 7 have published drafts 🔴 7 haven't started. Germany is one of them. No country took a formal step this week. A few things still changed. → Greece. Since Sunday, applicants get pay information before the first interview, and pay-history questions are banned. → Poland. From Thursday, employers carry the burden of proof in unequal pay claims. → Netherlands. The bill gets a committee debate on 14 Dec. We said 7 Dec last week. That was wrong, and the tracker now says so. → Germany. A secondary source now says the cabinet decision is planned for December. That date has moved six times. One thing to do this week. Open your job ad template and your application form. Add a pay range. Delete any question about current or past salary. Greece needs that now. Germany will need it the day its law arrives. Tracker and the weekly email are in the first comment. #PayTransparency #EUPayTransparency #HR … more",
