      "id": "LI01020",
      "sourceId": "LI01020",
      "url": null,
      "text": "Your employer's PF contribution should not come at the cost of your take-home salary. A change in PF policy should strengthen employees' financial security — not silently reduce their monthly earnings. Following the revision of the EPF wage ceiling, many employees are seeing changes in their salary structures. But an important question needs to be asked: Can an employer shift its own statutory PF contribution burden onto employees by reducing their wages or salary components? Section 12 of the Employees' Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952, specifically restricts employers from reducing employees' wages or employment benefits merely because of their liability to pay PF contributions. Consider what employees are experiencing: - Fixed CTC remains unchanged. - Employer PF contribution is adjusted within the existing CTC. - Salary components such as Special Pay are reduced. - Monthly take-home salary goes down. For employees, this is not just a payroll adjustment. It directly affects monthly household budgets, rent, bills, savings and financial commitments. Retirement savings are important. But employees should not be made to bear the employer's statutory contribution through reduced wages. I believe every employer should provide: 1. A transparent revised CTC structure. 2. A clear explanation of employee and employer PF contributions. 3. Written justification for any reduction in salary components. 4. Confirmation that the restructuring complies with applicable labour laws. A social security reform should protect employees without compromising their present financial stability. Employees deserve transparency, fairness and respect for their hard-earned salaries. #EPF #PF #EmployeeRights #LabourLaws #SalaryStructure #CTC #TakeHomeSalary #India … more",
