      "id": "LI01062",
      "sourceId": "LI01062",
      "url": null,
      "text": "I run reesmarx europe, and for over 30 years we’ve helped US/ Asian and UK/European companies hire across the UK, Europe and Asia. In that time I’ve noticed the same few rules catch people out, again and again. Here are five worth knowing before you make your first hire: Germany: if you need to let someone go, the letter has to be on paper with a handwritten signature. An email or DocuSign won’t count. Germany: a non-compete only holds up if you pay the person at least half their salary for as long as it runs. Netherlands: if an employee is off sick, you could be paying at least 70% of their salary for up to two years. France: contracts and company policies need to be in French. English-only versions can come back to bite you. Across the EU: new pay transparency rules mean salary ranges up front and no asking candidates what they earned before. Each country is bringing this in on its own timetable, so it’s worth checking where you’re hiring. GDPR. Data rules apply to candidate data too, so even CVs held in a US-based applicant tracking system come under them. None of this should put anyone off. UK/Europe are great markets. But when companies take there first steps over here, getting the people and the local rules right from day one tends to become the hard part. That’s exactly where reesmarx europe help. If you’re thinking about building a team in UK/Europe, I’d be happy to have a chat. #InternationalHiring #Europe #MarketEntry … more",
