      "id": "LI01069",
      "sourceId": "LI01069",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7523981517308571648/",
      "text": "What's your perspective? #Please #Read #Below I received a call from an HR representative for a general discussion regarding a job opportunity. During the conversation, she asked about my salary expectations. I shared my expectations honestly and confidently. She then asked, \"Is it negotiable?\" I politely replied, \"No.\" She further asked why my expectations were not negotiable. My #response was simple: \"An employee is expected to follow company policies, maintain working hours, fulfil assigned responsibilities, and sometimes even go beyond their defined tasks when required. But if an employee wants to discuss working hours, additional responsibilities, or certain expectations, is the company equally open to negotiation? If mutual understanding is important from the employee's side, shouldn't it also be important from the employer's side?\" I completely understand that salary discussions are a normal part of the hiring process. Negotiation is also a professional practice. However, I believe negotiation should be based on mutual respect, transparency, and understanding — not just on the expectation that a candidate should always compromise. A job is not just about what an employee can offer to a company. It's also about what the company offers in return. Respect the candidate's expectations just as you expect candidates to respect the company's expectations. Because professional relationships should always work both ways. #SalaryNegotiation #HRPractices #Hiring #WorkplaceCulture #EmployeeValue #ProfessionalRespect #Recruitment … more",
